  "text": "Scenario: A hymn from the Rig Veda describes elaborate fire rituals with precise chants. A modern reader calls it 'just superstition around a campfire.'\nBackground: Following the decline of the Indus Valley cities, Indo-Aryan-speaking peoples became dominant across northern India from around 1500 BCE, a period known for the composition of the Vedas — the oldest Hindu scriptures, preserved through exact oral memorisation for centuries before ever being written down. This era established Sanskrit, the classical language of Hindu ritual and Indian scholarship, and developed early philosophical and religious ideas — including fire rituals believed to maintain cosmic order — that shaped Hindu practice for millennia afterward.\nLegacies: Sanskrit & the Vedas (Sanskrit is the root of many South Asian languages, and the Vedas are still recited in Hindu ritual today.) Early Hindu philosophical concepts (Ideas from this era still shape religious practice and thought across South and Southeast Asia.)\nKey terms: Vedic period: An era named after the Vedas, early Sanskrit religious texts. Ritual: A set of actions performed in a fixed, meaningful way, often religious. Oral tradition: Knowledge passed down by memorised speech rather than writing.\nWhat careful reasoning shows: This places the practice in its own worldview: a society valuing precise oral transmission and ritual accuracy for cosmic reasons — not mere 'campfire superstition.'"
