  "skill": "Causation vs. correlation",
  "text": "Scenario: A textbook claims the Gupta Empire's great advances in maths and astronomy happened simply because the Gupta kings were personally brilliant.\nBackground: The Gupta Empire (roughly 320-550 CE) is often called a golden age of Indian civilisation. Under royal patronage, scholars made major advances in mathematics — including the concept of zero as a number and the decimal place-value system still used worldwide — alongside astronomy, medicine, and the arts. The mathematician-astronomer Aryabhata calculated the length of the year with remarkable accuracy and proposed that the Earth rotates on its axis. Gupta rule also saw flourishing literature, sculpture, and relative political stability across northern India.\nLegacies: The numeral system, including zero (This is the basis of the number system the entire world uses today.) Advances in astronomy & medicine (Aryabhata's early scientific ideas fed into the broader global scientific tradition that followed.)\nKey terms: Patronage: Financial or political support given to scholars, artists, or institutions. Golden age: A period remembered for major cultural or scientific achievement. Astronomy: The scientific study of stars, planets, and space.\nWhat careful reasoning shows: This correctly identifies multiple documented contributing factors rather than crediting one person, avoiding the trap of oversimplified causation."
