  "text": "Scenario: A stone inscription at a Chola temple lists names, gifts, and dates of a village assembly's decisions. A visitor assumes it must be about a military victory.\nBackground: The Chola dynasty (roughly 850-1279 CE) ruled much of southern India and built a powerful navy that projected influence across the Bay of Bengal into Southeast Asia. Cholas were skilled administrators, recording detailed local governance through temple inscriptions describing village assemblies (sabhas) that managed land grants, irrigation, and local disputes — evidence of organised self-governance at the community level. Chola artisans were also famous for bronze sculpture, especially intricate images of Hindu deities, cast using a technique still practised in South India today.\nLegacies: Maritime trade networks (A historical precedent for the trade links between India and Southeast Asia that remain active today.) Village self-governance (assemblies) (An early form of local, semi-democratic administration, studied today as a historical precedent.)\nKey terms: Inscription: Words carved into a durable surface like stone or metal. Village assembly: A local governing body making community decisions, documented in Chola inscriptions. Maritime trade: Trade carried out by sea routes, a Chola strength.\nWhat careful reasoning shows: Correct close reading — the text itself describes local governance and land grants. This reveals the Cholas' sophisticated administrative system, a different and equally important legacy than military conquest."
