  "text": "Scenario: A jewelled dagger said to have belonged to Akbar has vanished from its case. A court chronicle calls him a uniquely wise, tolerant ruler beloved by all his subjects — is that the full picture?\nBackground: The Mughal Empire (1526-1857) ruled much of the Indian subcontinent, blending Persian, Central Asian, and Indian traditions into a distinctive court culture. Emperor Akbar expanded and consolidated the empire while pursuing policies of religious tolerance and administrative reform, including the mansabdari system that ranked officials by military and civil duty. Mughal patronage produced extraordinary architecture, including the Taj Mahal, and miniature painting that fused Persian artistic style with Indian subject matter — a lasting influence on South Asian art and architecture.\nLegacies: Architecture (Taj Mahal, Indo-Persian style) (A major UNESCO World Heritage Site and one of the world's most visited tourist attractions today.) Administrative system (mansabdari, multi-religious governance) (Influenced the administrative structures of later Indian states, including approaches to governing diverse populations.)\nKey terms: Court chronicle: An official record written for, or by order of, a ruler's court. Mansabdari system: The Mughal administrative system ranking officials by military and civil duties. Patron: A person, often powerful, who commissions and pays for a work.\nWhat careful reasoning shows: Correctly identifies the chronicle's built-in motive to flatter its patron — the core move of sourcing. Correctly identifies that an outsider isn't automatically neutral — his own agenda still needs to be accounted for before using his claims. Correctly identifies that a source's mundane, non-celebratory purpose makes it a valuable, less biased type of evidence for sourcing purposes. This correctly weighs each source by its type of bias, using the least motivated source (administration) to check the two more motivated accounts. You've solved it. Sourcing each account by its motive — and trusting routine records over celebratory ones — reveals a genuinely impressive administrative achievement, without repeating the court's own flattering claims as fact."
