  "text": "Scenario: An East India Company report describes a famine as an unavoidable natural disaster with no mention of company policy.\nBackground: The British East India Company began as a trading corporation in 1600 and, over two centuries, transformed into a political and military power controlling large parts of the Indian subcontinent — an unusual case of a commercial company acting as a government. Company rule reshaped land taxation, trade, and infrastructure, including early railway and canal networks. Its administration is now studied as an early example of the dangers of concentrated corporate power, and its policies during famines remain a subject of serious historical debate. Direct British Crown rule replaced Company rule in 1858.\nLegacies: Corporate-colonial governance (Studied today as an early case of corporate power needing regulation and accountability.) Institutions left behind (railways, legal/education systems) (Still form part of the infrastructure and English-medium education systems in the region today.)\nKey terms: East India Company: A British trading company that gained political and military control over large parts of India. Famine: A severe, widespread shortage of food. Administrative record: An official document produced to manage governance, trade, or resources.\nWhat careful reasoning shows: This correctly recognises the report's author has a direct interest in omitting its own responsibility, and seeks independent verification — sourcing applied to an institution, not just an individual."
