  "skill": "Causation vs. correlation",
  "text": "Scenario: A miniature terracotta warrior has gone missing from a museum case. A pamphlet claims Qin Shi Huang built the entire army purely out of personal vanity — no other explanation needed.\nBackground: Qin Shi Huang unified China's Warring States into a single empire in 221 BCE, becoming its first emperor. His short but transformative reign standardised writing, currency, weights, and measurements across the newly unified territory, and connected earlier defensive walls into what became the basis of the Great Wall. Qin rule was built on Legalism, a philosophy emphasising strict laws and centralised control, which relied on large-scale organised labour conscription for major state projects — including the emperor's enormous tomb complex, guarded by the now-famous Terracotta Army.\nLegacies: Standardisation (script, currency, measurements) (A precedent for the unified national systems of writing, money, and measurement countries still use today.) Terracotta Army & early Great Wall (Major UNESCO World Heritage tourism sites, drawing millions of visitors to China today.)\nKey terms: Causation: One event or factor directly bringing about another. Correlation: Two things occurring together, without one necessarily causing the other. Legalism: A Qin-era philosophy emphasising strict laws and centralised state control.\nWhat careful reasoning shows: Correct — a source's distance in time and its author's own political context both matter before accepting its explanation of motive. This reads the archaeological evidence for exactly what it shows — scale and organisation — without assuming it proves one specific psychological motive. This correctly identifies a real, documented mechanism (the Qin state's labour system) rather than treating the tomb as an isolated act of personal vanity. This correctly integrates all three: mechanism, scale, and a cautiously-weighed later account, avoiding both the 'pure vanity' claim and an unchecked acceptance of Sima Qian. You've solved it. Separating a real documented mechanism (the labour system) from an unproven single motive is exactly what causation-vs-correlation reasoning requires — and it's a habit worth applying to any 'one simple cause' claim about history."
