  "text": "Scenario: A merchant's ledger from the Han dynasty records goods moving along Silk Road routes. A report claims 'nothing about Chinese trade changed' across the whole dynasty.\nBackground: The Han dynasty (206 BCE - 220 CE) followed the short-lived Qin and ruled for roughly four centuries, becoming a defining era of Chinese civilisation — Chinese people still often call themselves the 'Han people' today. The Han adopted Confucianism as state philosophy and established an early civil-service examination system based on merit rather than birth alone. Han rule also saw the opening of the Silk Road, extensive trade networks connecting China to Central Asia, South Asia, and eventually the Mediterranean, carrying silk, paper, and other goods across vast distances.\nLegacies: The Silk Road (A historical precedent for today's global trade and connectivity networks spanning continents.) Confucian civil-service exams (Merit-based hiring through examination, echoed in modern civil service systems around the world.)\nKey terms: Continuity: What stays the same across a period of time. Change: What transforms or shifts across a period of time. Silk Road: Trade networks connecting China to Central Asia, South Asia, and beyond.\nWhat careful reasoning shows: This correctly separates continuity (core goods) from change (expanding routes and volume) instead of flattening the whole era into one or the other."
