  "text": "Scenario: A Tang court poem praises the emperor in extravagant, flowery language. A modern reader calls it 'obviously fake flattery, so worthless as history.'\nBackground: The Tang dynasty (618-907 CE) is widely regarded as a golden age of Chinese culture, marked by political strength, territorial expansion, and extensive trade along the Silk Road that brought merchants, monks, and ideas from Central Asia, Persia, and beyond into a genuinely cosmopolitan capital. Tang poetry, including the work of Li Bai and Du Fu, remains among the most celebrated literature in Chinese history, and the era's openness to foreign trade and religion shaped neighbouring Japan and Korea, which adopted Tang administrative models, writing, and culture.\nLegacies: Golden age of poetry (Tang poetry is still core to Chinese literary education today.) Cosmopolitan trade & cultural exchange (Shaped the culture of neighbouring Japan and Korea, whose writing and institutions still reflect this influence.)\nKey terms: Tang poetry: Poetry from the Tang dynasty, a celebrated era of Chinese literary achievement. Court poet: A poet formally attached to and often employed by a royal court. Literary convention: An accepted stylistic tradition understood within its own culture and era.\nWhat careful reasoning shows: This correctly situates the poem within Tang literary convention, extracting real historical value (insight into court culture) without treating it as literal fact."
